Q: GoogleJsonResponseException: API call to drive.files.update failed with error: The user does not have sufficient permissions for this file A: <p>Check your <strong>OAuth Scopes</strong>.</p>
<p>If your script only has the <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file</code> scope, it can only touch files that the script itself created. Since you're iterating through your entire Drive, you need the full <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive</code> scope. You can check this in your Project Settings under &quot;show appsscript.json manifest file.&quot;</p>
<p>Second, if any of these files are located in a <strong>Shared Drive</strong> (or just Team Drives), the <code>Drive.Files.update</code> call will fail unless you explicitly tell it to support all drives.</p>
<p>i tried to adjust your version:</p>
<pre><code>function updateFilePermissions() {
  var email = Session.getActiveUser().getEmail();
  var files = DriveApp.getFiles();

  while (files.hasNext()) {
    var file = files.next();
    var fileID = file.getId();

    try {
      var owner = file.getOwner();
      // Added a null check because Shared Drive files can return null for getOwner()
      if (owner &amp;&amp; email === owner.getEmail()) {
        // We include 'supportsAllDrives' for compatibility
        Drive.Files.update(
          { copyRequiresWriterPermission: true }, 
          fileID, 
          null, 
          { supportsAllDrives: true }
        );
        Logger.log('Updated: ' + file.getName());
      }
    } catch (e) {
      Logger.log('Failed on file ' + file.getName() + ': ' + e.toString());
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: how to change the color of an image in a HTML5 Canvas without changing its pattern A: <p>You can combine filters on a single line of code before performing your draw operation, like this:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const canvas = document.getElementById('canvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
const image = document.getElementById('source');

ctx.filter = 'hue-rotate(120deg) grayscale(10%) brightness(150%)';
ctx.drawImage(image, 10, 10, 180, 120);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;canvas id="canvas"&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
&lt;div style="display:none;"&gt;
  &lt;img id="source" src="https://i.sstatic.net/Kk1qd.png"&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
